Abstract

We develop a formal theory of sequential payload deployment from reusable launch vehicles (RLVs), instanced on the SpaceX Starship "Pez Dispenser" system. The central thesis is that deployment authorization is not a mechanical event but an epistemic decision: it must be taken under imperfect, correlated, and state-dependent observability, and its authority must not be inherited across payloads. We construct a six-layer architecture whose formal core is a Deployment Safety Kernel (DSK) that produces authorization decisions from evidence-theoretic belief functions defined on a four-element frame of discernment $\Theta = \{\text{SAFE}, \text{MECH}, \text{THERM}, \text{DEB}\}$. We replace the naive correlation adjustment by a signed-dependence credal set that treats positive and negative residual correlations asymmetrically; replace the single Bel/Pl decision by a worst-case decision over the induced credal set, solvable by linear programming; and formalize the sequential process in TLA⁺ with an adversarial Environment action that can force any action precondition false. The theory is presented with all known defects logged, all external factual claims tracked in a claim ledger, and all open problems enumerated. The dissertation is released under an explicit falsification protocol: no LLM is permitted to repair an identified flaw silently, and every critique must produce a (Claim, Counterexample, Severity, Required Revision) tuple. The defensible core — sequential epistemic authorization with per-payload reset, conflict/dependence separation, non-inheritable kernel authority, and adversarial environment as the correct TLA⁺ discipline — is argued to survive sustained adversarial review. Everything downstream is presented as a candidate for further development, not as a verified result.

