Chapter 2 — Background and Related Work

2.1 Separation as a Hazard Class

Guernsey et al. (AAS 03-071) report that from 1983 to 2005, spacecraft and fairing separation systems accounted for approximately 10% of all launch failures. This establishes separation as a non-negligible hazard class and motivates treating deployment as a first-class safety problem rather than a mechanical subroutine.

2.2 Redundancy and Common-Cause Failure

NASA HR-31 requires that the space system "provide at least single failure tolerance to catastrophic events, with specific levels of failure tolerance and implementation (similar or dissimilar redundancy) derived via an integration of the design and safety analysis." The standard further notes that "redundancy alone does not meet the intent of this requirement. When a critical system fails because of improper or unexpected performance due to unanticipated conditions, similar redundancy can be ineffective at preventing the complete loss of the system." Dissimilar redundancy "can be very effective provided there is sufficient separation among the redundant legs."

The most insidious failure mode, per NASA guidance, is software common-mode failure: "every instance of the same software image executing on multiple devices encounters the same unexpected condition simultaneously." This motivates our treatment of the DSK as a kernel requiring dissimilar deployment and an independent hardware monitor.

2.3 Formal Methods in Space Systems

NASA's Gateway Vehicle System Manager employs assume-guarantee contracts implemented in PlusCal/TLA⁺, with functionality represented as state machines and contracts evaluated as temporal formulas by model checkers such as TLC. Runtime assume-guarantee contracts are implemented in R2U2 as a runtime monitor. Assume-guarantee contracts define expected behavior of systems, subsystems, and components, with assumptions or pre-conditions on the subsystem's inputs bounding the conditions in which the contract applies. DLR has demonstrated symbolic model checking scalability to 250 state machines.

We adopt this discipline: the DSK specification is written in TLA⁺, with contracts scoping the evidential layer, and with an adversarial Environment action to ensure that invariants are non-vacuous.

2.4 Evidence Theory, Conflict, and Dependence

Dempster–Shafer theory (DST) requires independence: classical evidence theory assumes that bodies of evidence are independent, and Dempster's rule can only be applied to independent bodies of evidence. The literature distinguishes two orthogonal pathologies:

· Conflict: mutually inconsistent evidence, addressed by PCR5/PCR6.
· Dependence: statistically dependent sources, addressed by random-set theory, copulas, or β-factor decomposition.

The PCR5 and PCR6 rules are not associative. The sequential combination of $S > 2$ BBAs is not in general equal to the global combination because the order of combination matters. PCR6 is preferred over PCR5 for $S > 2$ because it is more stable and simpler to implement, and is quasi-associative.

2.5 Random Sets and Copulas for Belief-Function Joining

Random sets are random variables whose values are sets; they induce a set of probability measures whose lower and upper probabilities correspond to belief and plausibility functions. Copulas can describe dependence between random sets. A joint belief function is related to its marginal belief functions by a family of copulas; a single copula is generally insufficient. The correct construction replaces cumulative distribution functions by cumulated masses: the joint mass is computed as the $C$-volume of the copula on the partition of the unit hypercube generated by the marginal focal elements. Applying the copula directly to lower probabilities does not produce a coherent lower probability.

2.6 Gap

Within the reviewed literature, no single framework jointly addresses (a) sequential constellation deployment, (b) evidence-theoretic authorization under correlated observability with explicit conflict–dependence separation, (c) a formally verified safety kernel with non-inherited authorization, and (d) constellation-aware state mutation with uncertainty propagation. This dissertation addresses the gap by construction, and documents what remains open.

