3.4 Inertia Mutation with Uncertainty Propagation

Between deployments $k$ and $k+1$, the correct mutation accounts for the removal of a finite mass $m_s$ at body-frame position $r_s$ with intrinsic inertia $I_s^{\text{cm}}$ about its own center of mass.

Step 1 — Translate removed-satellite inertia to old CG.

\mathbf{I}_s^{\text{old}} = \mathbf{I}_s^{\text{cm}} + m_s \big[ (\mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}}^\top \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}}) \mathbf{1} - \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}} \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}}^\top \big], \quad \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}} = \mathbf{r}_s - \mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}}.

Step 2 — Subtract from total inertia about old CG.

\mathbf{I}_{\text{remaining}}^{\text{old}} = \mathbf{I}_{\text{CG}} - \mathbf{I}_s^{\text{old}}.

Step 3 — Translate remaining body to new CG.

\mathbf{r}'_{\text{CG}} = \frac{m \mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}} - m_s \mathbf{r}_s}{m'}, \qquad m' = m - m_s,

\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}} - \mathbf{r}'_{\text{CG}} = \frac{m_s (\mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}} - \mathbf{r}_s)}{m - m_s},

\mathbf{I}'_{\text{CG}} = \mathbf{I}_{\text{remaining}}^{\text{old}} + m' \big[ (\mathbf{d}^\top \mathbf{d}) \mathbf{1} - \mathbf{d} \mathbf{d}^\top \big].

Uncertainty propagation. Let $\theta = (m_s, r_s, \text{vec}\, I_s^{\text{cm}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{10}$ with covariance $\Sigma_\theta$. Write $F(\theta) = \text{vec}\, I'_{\text{CG}}$ as a composition $F = T \circ S$, where

S(m_s, r_s, I_s^{\text{cm}}) = I_{\text{remaining}}^{\text{old}}, \qquad T(I, d) = I + m'[(d^\top d)\mathbf{1} - d d^\top].

The Jacobian is

J_{I'} = \frac{\partial \text{vec}\, T}{\partial \text{vec}\, I} \cdot \frac{\partial \text{vec}\, S}{\partial \theta} + \frac{\partial \text{vec}\, T}{\partial d} \cdot \frac{\partial d}{\partial \theta},

with $\partial \text{vec}\, T / \partial \text{vec}\, I = I_9$, and with the remaining blocks computable in closed form from the parallel-axis expressions. The second-order remainder is bounded by

\|R_2\| \leq \tfrac{1}{2} \left\| \frac{\partial^2 T}{\partial d^2} \right\| \cdot \|\Sigma_\theta\|_F \cdot m'.

Assumption. The body-frame origin coincides with the pre-mutation vehicle CG for the purpose of measuring $r_s$. If the dispenser indexes between payloads, the dispenser's own kinematic state must be added to $\theta$. Logged as Open Problem 10.13.

Status. Formal, not operational. No public probabilistic model of $(m_s, r_s, I_s^{\text{cm}})$ exists for a flatellite. This is Open Problem 10.8.

