Chapter 4 — Epistemic Framework

4.1 Frame of Discernment

\Theta = \{\text{SAFE}, \text{MECH}, \text{THERM}, \text{DEB}\},

with the four atoms mutually exclusive and exhaustive in the DST reading. In the DSmT free model, exclusivity is dropped and paradoxical masses are permitted; PCR6 is then computed on free-model focal sets. Ignorance is represented by mass on $\Theta$ itself, not by a distinguished atom. This is a correction of earlier drafts in which a fifth atom "IGN" was introduced; that construction is non-standard and has been abandoned.

Let $\text{HAZ} = \Theta \setminus \{\text{SAFE}\}$. Belief and plausibility are defined as usual:

\text{Bel}(A) = \sum_{B \subseteq A} m(B), \qquad \text{Pl}(A) = \sum_{B \cap A \neq \emptyset} m(B).

4.2 Observation-to-Mass Mapping

Each source $i$ observes $O_i \in \mathcal{O}_i$ drawn from a distribution parameterized by the current plant state:

O_i \sim \pi_i(\cdot \mid \Sigma^{(k)}), \qquad m_i = g_i(O_i; \Sigma^{(k)}).

The state-dependence is essential: between deployments $k$ and $k+1$, the mass, inertia, and thermal state of the vehicle mutate via Chapter 3, and the residual covariance matrices $\Sigma_{O_i, O_j}$ update accordingly. Any framework that treats the observation-to-mass map as static ignores the fact that the plant itself is time-varying during sequential deployment.

4.3 Residual Correlation

Let $\bar{O}_i(\Sigma^{(k)})$ be the predicted observation from a nominal model parameterized by the current state. Define the residual

r_i = O_i - \bar{O}_i(\Sigma^{(k)})

and the residual correlation

\rho_{ij}^{(k)} = \frac{\text{Cov}(r_i, r_j \mid \Sigma^{(k)})}{\sigma_{r_i} \sigma_{r_j}}.

This is a property of the joint distribution of residuals, not of the mass functions directly. It is well-defined because residuals are random variables.

4.4 Signed-Dependence Credal Set

Positive and negative correlation channels are separated:

\rho_{ij}^+ = \max(\rho_{ij}^{(k)}, 0), \qquad \rho_{ij}^- = \max(-\rho_{ij}^{(k)}, 0).

Positive channel. For $\rho_{ij}^+ > \rho_{\text{th}}$, common-mode error reduces effective source diversity. The conservative credal set is the lower envelope

\mathcal{B}_{ij}^+ = \big\{ \text{Bel} : \text{Bel}(A) \geq (1 - \rho_{ij}^+) \text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A) \; \forall A \subseteq \Theta \big\}.

Negative channel. For $\rho_{ij}^- > \rho_{\text{th}}$, anti-correlated residuals constrain the joint and can tighten the independent fusion. The credal set is the upper envelope

\mathcal{B}_{ij}^- = \left\{ \text{Bel} : \text{Bel}(A) \leq \min\left(1, \frac{\text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A)}{1 - \rho_{ij}^-}\right) \; \forall A \subseteq \Theta \right\}.

Joint credal set. For $S$ sources,

\mathcal{B} = \bigcap_{i<j} \left( \mathcal{B}_{ij}^+ \cap \mathcal{B}_{ij}^- \right).

Proposition 4.1. For every $\rho_{ij} \in [-1, 1]$, the joint credal set $\mathcal{B}$ is non-empty and contains $\text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}$.

Proof. At $\rho_{ij} = 0$, both envelopes collapse to $\{\text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}\}$. For $\rho_{ij}^+ > 0$, $\text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A) \geq (1 - \rho_{ij}^+) \text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A)$ since $\rho_{ij}^+ \geq 0$. For $\rho_{ij}^- > 0$, $\text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A) \leq \text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A)/(1 - \rho_{ij}^-)$ since $1/(1 - \rho_{ij}^-) \geq 1$. The intersection over all pairs therefore contains $\text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}$. $\blacksquare$

Interpretation. Positive correlation destroys information, so it can only lower belief in SAFE. Negative correlation constrains the joint, so it can raise belief in SAFE. The signed treatment captures both. The absolute-value heuristic of earlier drafts collapsed the two directions and is rejected.

4.5 Copula-Based Combination

Let $m_1, m_2$ be marginal BBAs with focal sets ordered canonically by ascending cardinality, then lexicographically on the characteristic vector of each focal set. Let $F_1, F_2$ be the cumulative masses. For a copula $C_\theta$ on $[0,1]^2$, the joint mass on $A_k \times B_\ell$ is the $C$-volume

m_{12}^{C_\theta}(A_k, B_\ell) = \Delta_{F_1(A_{k-1})}^{F_1(A_k)} \Delta_{F_2(B_{\ell-1})}^{F_2(B_\ell)} C_\theta(u, v),

and the marginalized BBA is

m_{12}^{C_\theta}(Z) = \sum_{A \cap B = Z} m_{12}^{C_\theta}(A, B).

The canonical ordering is fixed to eliminate permutation ambiguity; the construction remains sensitive to changes in the cardinality ordering, which is why the ordering rule is specified rather than left implicit.

Family of copulas. In general, a single copula is not sufficient to characterize a joint belief function. The result is a family

\mathcal{B}_C = \big\{ \text{Bel}_{C_\theta} : \theta \in \Theta_{\text{copula}} \big\},

and this family enters the decision rule as part of the joint credal set.

4.6 Independence Regime

When independence holds, Dempster's rule applies:

(m_i \oplus m_j)(A) = \frac{1}{1 - K} \sum_{B \cap C = A} m_i(B) m_j(C), \qquad K = \sum_{B \cap C = \emptyset} m_i(B) m_j(C).

4.7 PCR6 in the High-Conflict Regime

For $S \geq 2$ sources with BBAs $m_1, \ldots, m_S$ on $\Theta$, the PCR6 rule is

m_{\text{PCR6}}(X) = m_{\cap}(X) + \sum_{i=1}^{S} \sum_{\substack{(X_1, \ldots, X_S) \in (2^\Theta)^S \\ \cap_{k=1}^{S} X_k = \emptyset \\ X_i \cap \big(\cup_{k \neq i} X_k\big) \neq \emptyset \\ X = X_i \cap \big(\cup_{k \neq i} X_k\big)}} \frac{m_i(X_i)^2 \prod_{k \neq i} m_k(X_k)}{\sum_{k=1}^{S} m_k(X_k)},

where

m_{\cap}(X) = \sum_{\cap_k X_k = X} \prod_{k=1}^{S} m_k(X_k).

The rule is from the standard DSmT literature (Smarandache–Dezert, Advances and Applications of DSmT, Vol. 3).

Complexity. The outer sum has $2^{|\Theta|}$ terms; the inner double sum is over $\binom{2^{|\Theta|}}{S}$ tuples. Total cost is $O\big(2^{|\Theta|} \cdot (2^{|\Theta|})^S\big)$, exponential in both $|\Theta|$ and $S$. For $|\Theta| = 4$, $S = 6$: roughly $10^7$ operations per fusion, tractable. For $|\Theta| = 4$, $S = 26$: roughly $4 \times 10^{28}$, intractable.

Approximation policy. For $S > S_{\text{max}} = 6$, we use sequential pairwise PCR6 in a fixed index order, with the documented loss of associativity. The order is logged for audit. The vacuous BBA is not a neutral element for $S > 2$; sources with $m_i(\Theta) > 1 - \epsilon$ are excluded by a gating rule before fusion.

4.8 Conflict-and-Correlation Measure

\Gamma(O) = w_K \frac{K}{K_{\text{max}}} + w_\rho \max_{i<j} \frac{|\rho_{ij}|}{\rho_{\text{max}}}, \qquad w_K + w_\rho = 1.

ABORT is triggered when $\Gamma(O) \geq \Gamma_{\text{max}} \in (0, 1]$.

4.9 Decision Rule

The Deployment Safety Kernel produces

A = \begin{cases}
\text{AUTHORIZE} & \inf_{\text{Bel} \in \mathcal{B}} \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE}) \geq \theta_N \;\land\; \sup_{\text{Bel} \in \mathcal{B}} \text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) \leq \theta_H \;\land\; \sup_{\text{Bel} \in \mathcal{B}} m(\Theta) \leq \theta_U \\[4pt]
\text{INHIBIT} & \inf_{\text{Bel} \in \mathcal{B}} \text{Bel}(\text{HAZ}) \geq \theta_H \\[4pt]
\text{HOLD} & \neg \text{AUTHORIZE} \;\land\; \neg \text{INHIBIT} \;\land\; t_{\text{hold}}(i) < t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}}(i) \\[4pt]
\text{ABORT} & t_{\text{hold}}(i) \geq t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}}(i) \;\lor\; \Gamma(O) \geq \Gamma_{\text{max}}
\end{cases}

where $\mathcal{B}$ is the joint credal set of Section 4.4 intersected with the copula-induced family $\mathcal{B}_C$ of Section 4.5.

The worst-case structure is the correct decision-theoretic response to a credal set: authorization requires that every compatible belief function satisfy the thresholds. If any compatible belief function fails, authorization is not granted.

Computational structure. $\inf_{\text{Bel} \in \mathcal{B}} \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE})$ is a linear program over the credal set: constraints are linear in the mass vector, objective is linear. The same holds for $\sup \text{Pl}(\text{HAZ})$ and $\sup m(\Theta)$. The rule is therefore polynomial-time solvable, at the cost of one LP per decision.

4.10 Non-Vacuity Theorem

Theorem 4.1. If $m(\Theta) > 0$ and $\theta_N + \theta_H < 1$, then $\text{Bel}(\text{SAFE}) \geq \theta_N$ does not imply $\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) \leq \theta_H$.

Proof. $\text{Bel}(\text{SAFE}) = m(\text{SAFE}) \leq 1 - m(\Theta) < 1$. $\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) = m(\text{HAZ}) + m(\Theta) = 1 - m(\text{SAFE})$. If $\text{Bel}(\text{SAFE}) \geq \theta_N$, then $\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) \leq 1 - \theta_N$. The implication $\text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) \leq \theta_H$ holds iff $1 - \theta_N \leq \theta_H$, i.e. $\theta_N + \theta_H \geq 1$. When $\theta_N + \theta_H < 1$, the implication fails. $\blacksquare$

Corollary. $\theta_N + \theta_H < 1$ is necessary for the decision rule to be non-vacuous. The strict inequality (as opposed to the non-strict form of earlier drafts) is required because at $\theta_N + \theta_H = 1$ the two conjuncts coincide and the second is redundant.

4.11 HOLD Bound

For payload $i$,

t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}}(i) = \min\!\left( \frac{E_{\text{avail}}(i) - E_{\text{reserve}}}{P_{\text{hold}}}, \;\; \frac{T_{\text{max}} - T_{\text{current}}(i)}{\dot{T}_{\text{net}}}, \;\; T_{\text{window}}(t, i) \right) - \epsilon,

clamped to $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$, with guards for $P_{\text{hold}} = 0$ and $\dot{T}_{\text{net}} \leq 0$. The bound is recomputed continuously during HOLD.

