Chapter 8 — Methodological Commitments and the Adversarial Protocol

8.1 Why a Falsification Protocol

The development of this dissertation proceeded through four rounds of adversarial review (v0.2, v0.3, v0.4, v1.0, v2.0, v3.0). Each round identified defects that earlier rounds had introduced or failed to catch. Several of the "corrections" introduced new defects. This is a recognizable failure mode of iterative self-revision, and it is the reason the present document adopts an explicit falsification protocol rather than claiming correctness.

The protocol has three commitments:

1. No silent repair. Every identified flaw must be logged in the attack matrix with its status, severity, and required revision.
2. Mandatory counterexample format. Every critique must produce $(\text{Claim}, \text{Counterexample}, \text{Severity}, \text{Required Revision})$.
3. Independent review. Critiques are solicited from independent systems (Claude, Gemini, DeepSeek, Grok, Qwen, and others). No single system is permitted to adjudicate its own critique.

