Chapter 10 — Conclusion

We have presented a theoretical architecture for sequential payload deployment from reusable launch vehicles, formalized as a safety-preserving state-transition problem under imperfect, correlated, and evolving observability. The theory commits to specific formal choices — a four-element frame of discernment, a signed-dependence credal set, a worst-case decision rule over the joint credal set, a per-payload authorization reset, an adversarial TLA⁺ environment — and it documents every choice as attackable.

The dissertation's contribution is not a verified system. It is a falsification target. The falsification protocol of Chapter 8 governs all further review. No LLM is permitted to repair an identified flaw silently. Every critique must produce $(\text{Claim}, \text{Counterexample}, \text{Severity}, \text{Required Revision})$. Only after sustained adversarial destruction — by Claude, Gemini, DeepSeek, Grok, Qwen, and other independent systems — can the propositions that remain standing be considered candidates for further development.

The propositions that do not survive are, by design, discarded. The theory is offered in that spirit.

